  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening leadership</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Kiribati Australia Skills for Employment Program partners with Kiribati Institute of Technology (KIT) to provide vocational training and qualifications to I-Kiribati to build a skilled employable workforce meeting demands of domestic and regional labour markets. KIT&#x2019;s aged care course, Certificate III in Individual Support, enabled 154 I-Kiribati (138 women, 16 men) to work in aged care in Australia through labour mobility scheme, with further 50 graduates scheduled to depart. The course provides direct pathway to labour mobility and regional employment for women, who have previously been given small percentage of positions. In 2023, women accounted for 90% aged care completions or participated in aged care programs, supporting gender equality outcomes in Kiribati&#x2019;s education, training, and workforce participation. This course is highly demanded with over 6000 applicants awaiting opportunities to participate. Graduate aged care workers are highly sought after in Australia with continuous demand for new cohorts.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Kiribati Australia Skills for Employment Program (KASfEP)  supports the Ministry of Employment and Human Resources (MEHR) to strengthen its leadership and accountability practices through the Strategic Program Advisory Group (SPAG) which oversees strategic planning in skills and labour mobility. In 2023, KASfEP supported MEHR to revise the SPAG Terms of Reference to better enable MEHR to bring together and coordinate stakeholders engaged in labour mobility, skills and the employment sector in Kiribati. The twice-yearly SPAG meetings provide a platform for MEHR to update development partners on
&#x200d;
the Government of Kiribati&#x2019;s (GoK) priorities in skills and labour mobility; seek technical inputs and advice from partners; lead planning on the strategic direction of skills and labour mobility programs; and identify opportunities for enhanced collaboration with stakeholders. This is supporting the GoK&#x2019;s vision to enhance economic growth and prosperity through a strong skills and employment sector.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Kiribati Australia Skills for Employment Program strengthened the Ministry of Employment and Human Resource&#x2019;s (MEHR) capacity to coordinate stakeholders in the skills sector. In 2022, MEHR launched the Kiribati National Skills and Employment Sector Reference Group, with technical support from Australia. The Reference Group provides a forum for key stakeholders (ministries, private sector, NGOs and development partners) to discuss and address strategic issues and challenges affecting the Kiribati Technical, Vocational, Education and Training (TVET) sector. Through the Reference Group, MEHR is better positioned to ensure activities in the TVET sector contributes to the Government of Kiribati&#x2019;s development strategies.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Disability equity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Kiribati-Australia Skills for Employment Program (KASfEP), through the Kiribati Institute of Technology (KIT), supports Kiribati Association for the Blind and Visually Impaired (KABVI). Under a Memorandum of Understanding, KIT provides KABVI with opportunity to participate in disability inclusion designs for courses such as the customised Customer Service course for people with Vision Impairment while KABVI provides technical advice and guidance to KIT on disability awareness and inclusion. KIT benefited from KABVI&#x2019;s provision of disability awareness raising activities for staff and students; technical guidance on customising courses to better benefit and include people with disabilities: and support to develop and deliver KIT&#x2019;s Disability Support Plan, with a disability advisory role in KIT&#x2019;s Student Support Services team. Support to KABVI also resulted in  awareness sessions at KIT in partnership with the national eye clinic on importance of eye health and challenges experienced by people that have low vision or blind.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
